@startuml IsolatedBrowserProfiles
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: one browser choice across all defs with guest-owned profiles and an operator viewport
' status: proposed
' informed by: _spec/internal/sbx/browser-viewport.puml, _spec/internal/sbx/host-browser-cdp.puml
' informed by: _spec/internal/sbx/kit-lifecycle.puml, _spec/_plans/session-continuity.puml
' would touch: internal/run/choices.go, internal/run/run.go, internal/backend/sandbox/**, internal/sbx/run.go
' would touch: packages/lib/agent-browser, defs/hermes/entrypoint.sh, internal/imagebuild/targets.go
' would touch: e2e/browser_viewport_test.go
title PLANNED — Isolated browser profiles across all harnesses

actor "Operator" as OP
node "Operator's machine" {
  component "Proveo\nBrowser choice and run controls" as RUN
  component "Viewport\nmonitor, take control, return control" as VIEW
  database "Run receipt\nprofile name, sandbox ID, lease owner" as RECEIPT
}
node "sbx sandbox" {
  component "Run-scoped CDP relay" as RELAY
  component "Browser launcher\nagent-browser or Hermes attaches" as LAUNCH
  component "Chromium\nlocal CDP and sbx egress" as BROWSER
  database "Guest-private profile\ncookies and browser state" as PROFILE
}

OP ARROW_MAIN RUN : choose Browser
OP ARROW_MAIN VIEW : watch or sign in
RUN ARROW_MAIN RECEIPT : record profile and acquire lease
RUN ARROW_MAIN LAUNCH : resolve image, mode and ownership
VIEW ARROW_MAIN RELAY : per-run host loopback endpoint
RELAY ARROW_MAIN BROWSER : observe and interact with the same tabs
LAUNCH ARROW_MAIN BROWSER : start on first use and attach
BROWSER ARROW_MAIN PROFILE : read and write inside the sandbox

note bottom of RUN
  Offer one Browser choice across Claude Code,
  Codex, Cursor, OpenCode and Hermes. Off is the
  default for a new run. Isolated can use a fresh
  profile or a named profile. Host Chrome remains
  an advanced explicit option.
end note

note bottom of RECEIPT
  A named profile belongs to a specific sandbox.
  Keep its sandbox stopped between runs so the
  profile remains available. The workstation
  stores identity and lease metadata.
  Removing the sandbox removes the profile.
end note

note bottom of VIEW
  Monitoring and operator input use the same
  browser the agent sees. The operator can start
  the browser and sign in before giving control
  to the agent. Prevent both clients from driving
  the browser during an operator handoff.
end note

legend bottom
  **1. Resolve one browser choice**
  * Move the current browser and chrome controls into one choice. Preserve the existing CLI
    spellings and map remembered browser choices to Isolated. Keep existing explicit Host
    Chrome choices on the advanced path.
  * Offer fresh and named profiles under Isolated. Start the browser when the agent first
    needs it or the operator opens the viewport. Keep image selection behind the picker.
    Report the effective mode and retained profile in the run output.

  **2. Provision the same guest browser for every def**
  | Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, OpenCode | Use the pinned Chromium layer and attach agent-browser to the launcher's guest-local endpoint. |
  | Hermes TUI and Desktop | Reuse Hermes's bundled Chromium and set its native browser client to the same guest-local endpoint. |
  * Keep explicit image and variant tools available. Select a compatible browser layer or
    report a missing browser capability before launch.
  * Start one Chromium per sandbox profile using a guest-private user data directory.
    Keep browser traffic under sbx egress. Exclude the profile directory from workspace
    mounts and config sync.

  **3. Let the operator watch and sign in**
  * Reuse the host-to-sandbox relay and add a viewport action to the run's interface.
    Show tabs and popups and support pointer and keyboard input in operator control mode.
  * Support signing in before first agent use. For mid-run handoff, wait for in-flight browser
    actions and pause supported agent browser input until the operator returns control.
    The viewport's monitor mode suppresses input. The local CDP endpoint gives full control.
  * Publish the viewport on the workstation's loopback interface. Use the port sbx actually
    reports for a kept sandbox. Verify a WebSocket connection to the same browser from the
    guest and the host before reporting readiness. Close the relay when the run detaches or ends.

  **4. Keep named logins in sbx**
  * Stop the browser and delete fresh profile data during run cleanup, including when a
    harness retains an engine. A named profile survives by keeping its owning sandbox
    stopped. The host stores identity and lease metadata. Profile files stay in the guest.
  * Associate a named profile with its sandbox ID and resume it in that sandbox.
    Independent concurrent runs use distinct sandbox identities. Reattach joins its
    existing run and viewport.
  * Acquire one active lease per named profile. An existing lease gives the operator the
    choice to view the owning run or start with a different profile. Check the owner before
    releasing a stale lease. Confirm profile deletion when replacing or removing its sandbox.

  **5. Verify before delivery**
  * Drive the normal proveo entry point for all five defs and the Hermes Desktop path.
    Check lazy launch, host and guest seeing the same page, operator input, and resumed
    agent input. Use a controlled login fixture with a tab or popup transition.
  * Run parallel sandboxes with distinct cookies and verify no cross-run visibility. Stop
    and resume a named profile, verify its login survives, and confirm concurrent use is
    refused. Verify fresh teardown deletes the profile and closes its host endpoint.
  * Verify agent browsing remains subject to a restrictive sbx policy. Check port collisions
    and reattach, output under --print, and host-only sign-in flows that require Host Chrome.
endlegend
@enduml
